 CLASS IMBALANCE: When Data is Unbalanced
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

What is Class Imbalance?
  → One class has WAY more samples than others
  Example: 95% negative cases, 5% positive cases
           (Medical diagnosis where disease is rare)

Why is it a Problem?
  ✗ Model becomes biased toward majority class
  ✗ Accuracy metric is misleading
  ✗ Minority class predictions are ignored

Solutions:

1️⃣  UNDERSAMPLING / downsampling
    - Reduce the number of samples in the majority class so that it
      matches (or comes closer to) the minority class.
    • Remove samples from majority class
    • Pros: Faster training
    • Cons: Loss of data information
    code:
    from sklearn.utils import resample
    df_majority_downsampled = resample(df_majority,
                              replace=False, # sample without replacement
                              n_samples=len(df_minority), # to match minority class           
                              random_state=42)



2️⃣  OVERSAMPLING / upsampling
    - Increase the number of samples in the minority class so that it 
      matches (or comes closer to) the majority class.
    • Duplicate samples from minority class
    • Pros: Keep all data information
    • Cons: Risk of overfitting, slower training
    code:
    from sklearn.utils import resample
    df_minority_upsampled = resample(df_minority, 
                              replace=True, # sample with replacement
                              n_samples=len(df_majority), # to match majority class
                              random_state=42)
        

3️⃣  SMOTE (Synthetic Minority Over-sampling Technique)
    • Create synthetic samples of minority class
    • Best of both worlds
    • Most popular method
    code:

    from sklearn.datasets import make_classification
    X,y = make_classification(n_samples=1000, n_features=2, n_cluster_per_class=1, n_classes=2, weights=[0.95, 0.05], 
