═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

WHAT IS LOGISTIC REGRESSION?

Logistic Regression is used for CLASSIFICATION problems (despite its name!)
  • Despite "Regression" in name, it solves CLASSIFICATION problems
  • Predicts probability of a class (0 or 1, True or False)
  • Binary Classification (2 classes) is most common
  • Can be extended to multi-class classification

Key Idea: Map continuous values to probabilities between 0 and 1

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

🔍 HOW DOES LOGISTIC REGRESSION WORK?

STEP 1: LINEAR COMBINATION (Like Linear Regression)
  z = b + w₁x₁ + w₂x₂ + ... + wₙxₙ
  
  Where:
    z = weighted sum
    b = bias/intercept
    w = weights/coefficients
    x = features

STEP 2: SIGMOID FUNCTION (Squeeze to 0-1)
  
  Probability = 1 / (1 + e^(-z))
  
  This S-shaped curve converts any number to probability:
    • z = -∞ → probability = 0
    • z = 0 → probability = 0.5
    • z = +∞ → probability = 1

STEP 3: DECISION BOUNDARY
  
  If probability > 0.5 → Predict Class 1
  If probability ≤ 0.5 → Predict Class 0
  
  (You can adjust threshold, default is 0.5)

STEP 4: TRAINING (Find best w and b)
  
  Uses Gradient Descent to minimize loss function
  Loss function: Cross-Entropy Loss (Log Loss)
  Goal: Find weights that maximize correct predictions

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

📊 LOGISTIC REGRESSION vs LINEAR REGRESSION

┌────────────────────┬──────────────────────┬─────────────────────────┐
│ Aspect             │ Linear Regression    │ Logistic Regression     │
├────────────────────┼──────────────────────┼─────────────────────────┤
│ Output Type        │ Continuous values    │ Probability (0-1)       │
│ Problem Type       │ Regression           │ Classification          │
│ Function           │ Linear: y = mx + b   │ Sigmoid: P = 1/(1+e^-z) │
│ Loss Function      │ MSE (Mean Sq. Error) │ Cross-Entropy (Log Loss)│
│ Prediction         │ Any number           │ Class (0 or 1)          │
│ Use Case           │ Price, Temperature   │ Spam/Not Spam, Yes/No   │
└────────────────────┴──────────────────────┴─────────────────────────┘

