1️⃣5️⃣  HANDLING MISSING DATA
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

Types of Missing Data:

1️⃣  MISSING COMPLETELY AT RANDOM (MCAR)
    • No pattern to missing values
    • Safest to remove rows
    • Example: Random data entry error

2️⃣  MISSING AT RANDOM (MAR)
    • Missing depends on other variables
    • Can use those variables to estimate
    • Example: People with high income skip salary question (depends on income)

3️⃣  MISSING NOT AT RANDOM (MNAR)
    • Missing depends on value itself
    • Hardest to handle
    • Example: Rich people don't report income (high values missing)

Handling Strategies:

Option 1: DROP
  • Remove rows with missing values
  • Simple but loses data
  • Use when: <5% missing

Option 2: IMPUTATION
  a) Mean/Median/Mode
     • Replace with average value
     • Simple, fast
     • Loses information
     # mean -> is used when data is normally distributed
     # median -> is used when data has outliers
     # mode -> is used when data is categorical
  
  b) Forward/Backward Fill
     • Use previous/next value (for time series)
  
  c) K-NN Imputation
     • Use values from K nearest neighbors
     • Preserves relationships
  
  d) Regression Imputation
     • Predict missing value from other features
     • More sophisticated

Option 3: Create "Missing" Category
  • For categorical: Add "Unknown" category
  • For numerical: Create binary "is_missing" feature

